Found while marking the core API spec implemented for canlin#4-#16 (k10). Three stale spots in docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-23-canlin-core-api-design.md: (1) §6 at line 134 still says 'The existing canlin_sandbox_run only executes a chunk. The slice adds one module object' - the module object exists (include/canlin/module.h:131 has canlin_module_load, _deliver, _reload, _stop). (2) §6 at line 149 still says 'Inside the sandbox this needs canlin_sandbox_call(...)' - it exists (include/canlin/sandbox.h:103). (3) §7 at line 201 says the values are 'to be re-measured on real hardware (canlin#3)', while include/canlin/ev_limits.h and module_limits.h also cite canlin#3 but sandbox_limits.h cites canlin#13 - the calibration card whose blocker is the canlin-os QEMU harness. Decide which card the re-measurement belongs to and make the spec and the headers say the same. The doc-writer flagged these and did not touch them, because they are body text and not the status paragraph.

2026-11-14 23:41 Review 2026-11-14: fixed and reviewed. The core API spec §6 no longer reads forward-looking: it says what the code does - the module object owns a sandbox and a scheduler generation (include/canlin/module.h), the sandbox call is canlin_sandbox_call, a draw runs with the smaller budget of canlin_sandbox_call_budget, which is CANLIN_SB_BUDGET_DRAW, and the module table comes from canlin_sandbox_load_main while canlin_sandbox_run stays the bare-chunk entry. The worker checked each against the code (src/module/module.c:914 and :545). §7 now names the re-measurement per constant group, the way the headers already do: the CANLIN_EV_*, CANLIN_MODULE_*, CANLIN_TIMER_* and CANLIN_MARSHAL_* values are measured against the real P2 hardware (canlin#3, the hardware inventory of the two laptops), the CANLIN_SB_* budgets are calibrated on P2-QEMU (canlin#13, blocked on the canlin-os harness). That is exactly the split include/canlin/ev_limits.h and module_limits.h (canlin#3) versus sandbox_limits.h (canlin#13) already draw, so the spec and the headers say the same thing. The status paragraph was adjusted for the same reason. Committed as 9a20954. Named and not fixed: include/canlin/ev_limits.h says 'The spec table has no process-wide op/timer row', which is stale - §7 has had CANLIN_EV_PROC_MAX_OPS and CANLIN_EV_PROC_MAX_TIMERS rows since the table was written - now k134; and the specs write card ids as canlin#N while the project rule says kNN, which is now k135.
